"At the beginning of Surah as-Saffat, Allah swears by the angels lined up in ranks, those who drive forcefully, and those who recite the remembrance, emphasizing that your God is truly One. These oaths point to the importance of organized effort in Islam, such as community, jihad, and the pursuit of knowledge.\n\nThe lowest heaven has been adorned with stars and protected from every rebellious devil. The devils cannot listen to the exalted assembly, and those who attempt to eavesdrop are pursued by a piercing flame. This describes the material and spiritual defeat of those who remain heedless of divine revelation.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, the wrongdoers, their companions, and what they used to worship will be gathered and driven to Hell. There, they will blame one another: while the leaders say, 'You were not believers anyway,' the common people will reply, 'It was you who led us astray.' Ultimately, everyone will face retribution for what they have done.\n\nFor the people of Paradise, however, there are fruits, rivers, and noble companions. When one of them sees his worldly friend in Hell, he realizes that he was saved solely through the grace of Allah. The tree of Zaqqum, on the other hand, is the food of the dwellers of Hell; its shoots are as hideous as the heads of devils.\n\nThe prayer of Prophet Noah (Nuh) was answered; he and his family were saved from the deluge, and his lineage was perpetuated. A salutation of peace and an honorable reputation were preserved for him among later generations. Abraham (Ibrahim) was likewise among those who followed in his footsteps, turning to his Lord with a pure and sound heart.\n\nAbraham shattered the idols and confronted his people, proclaiming that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah. When they sought to cast him into the fire, Allah granted him aid; later, Abraham was tested with the sacrifice of his son Ishmael (Ismail). When both submitted, Allah ransomed him with a momentous sacrifice and characterized this event as a manifest trial. Afterward, the glad tidings of Isaac (Ishak) were given.\n\nAllah bestowed blessings upon Abraham, as well as upon Ishmael—the forbearing son—and upon Isaac. This blessing signified the multiplication and continuity of the progeny of both sons. Yet there is a crucial point here: from both lineages arose both righteous individuals and those who wronged their own souls. Thus, the piety of ancestors does not guarantee the righteousness of their descendants; wrongdoers emerged from the lineage of Abraham, just as idolaters emerged from the lineage of Noah. This underscores the primacy of personal faith and righteous deeds over ancestral lineage.\n\nNext, the story of Moses (Musa) and Aaron (Harun) is recounted. Allah favored them with blessings, delivered them and their people from immense distress, supported them, and made them victorious. He bestowed upon them the Scripture (Torah) and guided them to the straight path. An honorable memory was left for them among later generations, and it was said, \"Peace be upon Moses and Aaron.\" This demonstrates that the doers of good will be rewarded and that they were among Allah's believing servants.\n\nElijah (Ilyas) was also among the dispatched messengers. He exhorted his people not to worship the idol named Ba'l instead of Allah, yet they rejected him. A salutation of peace was likewise preserved for Elijah among subsequent generations. The Prophet Lot (Lut) is also mentioned; Allah saved him and his family, while annihilating the rest, except for an elderly woman who remained behind. It is noted that the Quraysh, traveling to Syria (Sham) for trade, passed by the ruins of Lot's people, serving as a cautionary lesson for them to take heed.\n\nThe story of Prophet Jonah (Yunus) contains profound lessons. Departed in anger with his people without divine leave, he fled aboard a laden ship; subsequently, lots were cast, he was cast into the sea, and was swallowed by a great fish. Within the belly of the fish, his prayer—\"There is no deity except You; exalted are You! Indeed, I have been of the wrongdoers\"—was accepted, and he was brought forth onto an open shore. A gourd-bearing plant was made to grow over him, and he was then sent back to a community of a hundred thousand or more. His people believed and were granted worldly sustenance for a time.\n\nAddressing the polytheists, the question \"Are the daughters for your Lord, while the sons are for them?\" censures their attribution of female offspring to Allah. While they regarded the angels as Allah's daughters, they themselves loathed having daughters. Furthermore, they fabricated a kinship between Allah and the jinn. Yet both the jinn and the angels know that these liars will be hauled into Hell. Exalted is Allah far above their claims. His sincere servants, however, never engage in such association (shirk). The polytheists and what they worship can only mislead those who are destined for Hell; they cannot lead the sincere servants astray.\n\nThe polytheists claim, \"If only we had possessed a scripture like earlier peoples, we would have been sincere servants.\" Yet when the Quran came to them, they disbelieved in it. In time, they will realize their fate. Allah has promised that He will aid His messengers and that His hosts will prevail. For this reason, the Prophet is commanded to turn away from them for an appointed time and observe what befalls them. The surah concludes by declaring the Lord far above the attributes ascribed to Him by the polytheists, extending peace upon the messengers, and offering all praise to Allah, the Lord of all the worlds."